1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,084
Buy now, pay later. Four easy payments. Zero interest.

2
00:00:04,504 --> 00:00:11,274
Used carefully, it really can cost nothing. But it's built to make spending feel smaller than it is. Let's run the numbers.

3
00:00:11,694 --> 00:00:21,778
Here's how it works. A $400 purchase becomes four payments of $100, every two weeks. Pay on time, and pay-in-4 plans often cost zero interest.

4
00:00:22,548 --> 00:00:24,449
So where's the catch? Three places.

5
00:00:25,219 --> 00:00:26,594
Catch one. Stacking.

6
00:00:27,014 --> 00:00:30,068
One plan is easy to track. But they add up quietly.

7
00:00:30,228 --> 00:00:31,606
Sneakers, $120.

8
00:00:31,766 --> 00:00:33,032
Headphones, $200.

9
00:00:33,192 --> 00:00:34,868
A grocery run, $160.

10
00:00:35,028 --> 00:00:36,280
And a gift, $280.

11
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:41,595
$760 in total, split into fours. That's $190 due every two weeks.

12
00:00:42,015 --> 00:00:47,823
In our example budget, fun money is a hundred per paycheck. These payments alone take almost double.

13
00:00:48,593 --> 00:00:50,732
Catch two. Fees when a payment misses.

14
00:00:51,152 --> 00:00:59,586
If autopay hits an empty account, you can get a late fee from the lender and an overdraft fee from your bank. Say $8, plus $35.

15
00:01:00,006 --> 00:01:05,747
$43 in fees on a $100 payment. Fees vary, so check your own lender and bank.

16
00:01:06,517 --> 00:01:08,720
Catch three. It makes things feel cheaper.

17
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:18,633
$100 sounds like a lot less than $400. That's the point. And some longer buy-now-pay-later loans do charge interest, so read which kind you're getting.

18
00:01:19,053 --> 00:01:20,447
So here's a simple rule.

19
00:01:20,607 --> 00:01:22,814
Only use it if you could pay in full today.

20
00:01:22,974 --> 00:01:24,990
If the payments fit inside your fun money.

21
00:01:25,150 --> 00:01:26,739
If it's your only active plan.

22
00:01:26,899 --> 00:01:29,475
And if autopay comes from an account that's funded.

23
00:01:30,245 --> 00:01:36,625
The Paycheck Planner in the description shows exactly what's free in each paycheck, before you split anything into four.

24
00:01:37,045 --> 00:01:40,217
Subscribe. Next time: pay off debt, or save first?
